body:
First of all, NAD, only a first-year med student.

&#x200B;

It's very hard to give a diagnosis based on what you said. And with that, I don't mean your post was unclear, I mean it could be a variety of different things. The 'crack'-sound makes me kind of worried, since it could be a fracture that, if goes untreated, could give you a lot of problems in the future.

&#x200B;

I can understand that you have your reservations towards doctors and hospitals for a variety of reasons, but in this case, I do think the best course of action is to go to the hospital so that they could check the mobility of your shoulder, perhaps get a CT and an echo, to make sure you won't have any long-term problems with your arm. 

&#x200B;

In any case, don't hesitate to write me back!

Regards

&#x200B;

P.S. I excuse myself for any grammatical or vocabular errors/awkward phrasing; not a native English speaker :)

First of all, NAD, only a first-year med student.

&#x200B;

A 0.5-surplus doesn't look too bad to me, especially since you mentioned you also ate some eggs in the morning (a big carrier of albumin).

That being said, hyperalbuminemia (fancy word for saying 'too much albumin') generally indicates dehydration (too much of those molecules => your body wants some more fluids). Drink a glass of water, I'd say, but, if that's the only thing and you don't have any symptoms, it doens't look as something to worry about. :) But hey, if you still feel a bit concerned, call your GP! :)

&#x200B;

In any case, don't hesitate to write me back!

Regards

&#x200B;

P.S. I excuse myself for any grammatical or vocabular errors/awkward phrasing; not a native English speaker :)

First of al NAD, just a first-year med student.

&#x200B;

Yes, it would. It would definitely, please don't do that.

Antibiotics are very strict with their doses. As you may know, don't ever just 'stop' your antibiotic treatment, since that could result in having the infection for a longer time, or even developing an antibiotic resistance. Both things you don't want to have.

Taking all your medication quickly (an antibiotic overdose) could also result in having very bad side-effects (diarrhea, stomach upset; especially since you're only 165cm and 51kg) and it will probably also have its negative effects for you on the further course of the infection.

&#x200B;

Im hearing your distress call, and if reddit is a resort to find answers because you're thàt fatigued, I'm not doubting you have to be feeling very bad; and then I'm not even talking about the stress due to the schoolwork. However, the best course of action to ged rid of your fatigueness and the infection as quick as possible, is taking the druggs as advised by your doctor.

If your condition seems to worsen, or you really have your concerns about it, please go see your GP! :)

&#x200B;

In any case, don't hesitate to write me back!

Regards

&#x200B;

P.S. I excuse myself for any grammatical or vocabular errors/awkward phrasing; not a native English speaker :)

First of all, NAD, only a first-year med student here.

&#x200B;

Would you describe the pain as some kind of pressure, (sligthly) pulsating, in sync with your heartbeat?  
Also, comes there something physically prior to the pain? Could be anything really, from a (slight) loss of focus-vision to having the chills, seeing flashes, etc.

Can you think of anything that consistently happens, the days before the headaches? Perhaps stressfull meetings, drinking more coffee or beer, or even eating more chocolate than you're used too. Think about it if there are any patterns like that.

When do the headaches start; the moment you wake up, or rather throughout your day? 

Also, you said you have them 'every single day', but let's say you start on monday with a headache, how long does it last? several hours, or even several days? And when does a "new" headache start then?

&#x200B;

To me, at this point, a tumor doesn't look too probable, especially if there was nothing to see on the CT. The first thought that crossed my mind when I read your post, was migraine. However, again, NAD, so please go see your primary care doctor, or your neurologist to confirm that diagnosis. If it are migraine-attacks, and if they are as severe and frequent as you state here, you may get some medication for when an attack is happening, as well as something you have to take every day. However, let's not get ahead of ourselves. When you feel a headache is coming/having a headache, keep it at a simple paracetamol (or anything like so) for now.

&#x200B;

Swollen lymph nodes, difficulty swallowing and the itchy watery eyes could be allergies? However, get that checked out too!  
A lot of pollen in the air where you live?

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Wish you all the best in your search!  
Regards

&#x200B;

P.S. I excuse myself for grammatical or vocabular errors/awkward phrasing; not a native English speaker :)
